/*
  `getting-started/first-scene` Part 5, file one of two. This is the scene the
  tutorial builds across Parts 1-4 (frontmatter, one shot, three content lines,
  the `orderChoice` branch) with Part 5's two additions: the `run.metMira`
  declaration and the `::set` at the end of The Counter. Nothing here is
  invented for the fixture -- every line is a block the page shows.

  It is the prerequisite half of the pair: `booth.lute` declares
  `after: 'visited("mira.s01ep01")'` and reads `run.metMira`, and the
  unconditional `::set` below is what makes that read Guaranteed under the
  project envelope. `lute check-project` proves it; single-file `lute check`
  on booth.lute alone cannot, which is exactly the point Part 5 makes.
*/

## The Counter

@narrator: The diner is empty at this hour, and Mira likes it that way.

@mira{emotion="content" variant="0"}: {{userName}}, you made it.

@mira{mono}: I should not be this pleased about a coffee order.

<branch id="orderChoice">
  <choice id="black" label="Order it black">
    @mira{emotion="content" variant="0"}: Good. No nonsense in a cup.
  </choice>
  <choice id="familiar" label="Say hi like an old friend" when="scene.knowsMira">
    @mira{emotion="surprised" variant="0"}: You remembered. That's new.
  </choice>
</branch>

::set{run.metMira = true}
